  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain moves out of the region this evening, with \npatchy drizzle and fog persisting overnight through Tuesday morning.\n\nSurface low pressure over Pennsylvania this evening will move to \nsouthern New England by Tuesday morning. The widespread rain from \nearlier today has exited to the east. Northerly low level upslope \nflow and leftover low level moisture will allow some drizzle to \npersist overnight, along with plenty of low clouds and patchy fog. \nThe fog and drizzle will likely be most prevalent across the higher \nterrain south of the NYS Thruway.\n\nSome patchy fog and drizzle may persist through mid morning Tuesday \nin some areas. High pressure will then build into the Great Lakes, \nwith drying and subsidence building into the area from northwest to \nsoutheast. This will bring an and to any lingering drizzle, and \nallow clouds to give way to increasing sunshine from northwest to \nsoutheast through the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Chances for showers and thunderstorms return \nThursday through the weekend.\n\nThe pattern will become active again starting Thursday as a mid \nlevel wave drops through a broad longwave trough and pushes a \nweak cold front into the region. Moisture returning on the back \nside of departing high pressure will support showers and possible\nthunderstorms as the cold front moves through on Thursday. \nAdditional, but lower chances for showers and thunderstorms will\ncontinue into the weekend as several ill-timed shortwave move \nthrough the region.",
